#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "89d0136d-63d1-540b-a3be-acff2d0bbbb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11027" :question/problem "For the function  $g(x,y,z) = 2 (x + 1)e^z+ yz,$  evaluate  $\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial z}$ at $(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x+1){e}^{z}+yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x+1){e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\  & =2(x+1)\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\  & =2(x+1){e}^{z}+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z}{|}_{(1,1,1)} & =2(1+1){e}^{1}+1 \\\\  & =4e+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "0fd784a0-5146-5f7b-9936-8d3bc43d7062" :question/math-academy-id "e-11028" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y) =x^2y\\ln{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =y\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =y\\cdot ((2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})) \\\\  & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & =xy(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "fc8ba431-5a97-5f57-9a83-fdcf4ac98d39" :question/math-academy-id "e-11048" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) =\\ln(y-x^2),$ evaluate $f_x(1,9).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot (y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot (y\\cdot 0-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,9)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,9) & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{9-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "6f7bdbcf-fb8e-5c70-b368-4e73cc94bdb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11112" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{4x-y}{x+y^2},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4x-y}{x+{y}^{2}}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)\\cdot (x+{y}^{2})-(4x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2})}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (x+{y}^{2})-(4x-y)\\cdot 1}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x+4{y}^{2}-4x+y}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{2}+y}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,-1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-1)} & =\\frac{4(-1)^{2}+(-1)}{{(2+{(-1)}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25bb17e4-258b-51bf-91d3-7e60cc8a5b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266482} {:answer/id #uuid "3b886ea8-78ac-5785-a126-630c13002539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266668} {:answer/id #uuid "90ef5240-d3e8-56bc-a359-94af08fe97cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267433} {:answer/id #uuid "d1666f3f-d78f-5b47-bf2e-cbd0ba0f6a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268015} {:answer/id #uuid "d92bf854-b63d-5ad5-821d-d64b45e82fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90ef5240-d3e8-56bc-a359-94af08fe97cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267433} :answer-field/id #uuid "c37a1b95-6721-5ce6-92db-42984b088eeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1798a630-b3d2-5681-be69-eeaa1d664d97" :question/math-academy-id "q-132253" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(3,-1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}{({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})}^{-1/2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}) & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot (2x+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,-1,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-1,-2)} & =\\frac{(3)(-1)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+4(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9+16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85813fa1-7598-522f-8c21-a78340a56373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380959} {:answer/id #uuid "794f355d-1fd3-5f85-acbc-2a0b9cb24276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380960} {:answer/id #uuid "d727996c-f099-52f9-bb09-3a563b0e8bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380961} {:answer/id #uuid "169f002f-fd26-50ae-930c-9a604e9ae96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380962} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d17484-2506-5d97-98a5-73a7fa14daee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1d17484-2506-5d97-98a5-73a7fa14daee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380963} :answer-field/id #uuid "391d0c46-ac45-548f-b507-5755302aa0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9414edde-aaa5-55fe-99cf-fcbd12cf83ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-132255" :question/problem "For the function $g(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y{z}^{2}),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂g}{∂z}$ at the point $(3,\\frac{1}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the constant multiple and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(y{z}^{2})}^{2}}})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}{z}^{4}}})\\cdot y\\cdot 2z \\\\  & =-\\frac{2yz\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}{z}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,\\frac{1}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z}{|}_{(3,\\,1/2,\\,1)} & =-\\frac{2(\\frac{1}{2})(1)\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(1)^{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1071740e-9558-5e73-b52c-d383864bf497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275622} {:answer/id #uuid "9e058e90-be98-5313-a164-e37b5eaec24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276896} {:answer/id #uuid "b028380b-a597-535a-bd93-8b4c6734b700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277053} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6cb830-da03-564b-9a88-50ef70b7374f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277142} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d0d589-d5cc-550b-974e-b4afdfaebb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e058e90-be98-5313-a164-e37b5eaec24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276896} :answer-field/id #uuid "75024153-b4e7-530d-be37-6a2d7adf8ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad571fe8-149e-54b6-a89f-66ae79db7481" :question/math-academy-id "q-159714" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x{y}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}y}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2h(x,y)}{{x}^{3}y}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{2x{y}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}y-(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)}{({x}^{2}y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}y-(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot 2xy}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2xy}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-2{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy(1-x{y}^{2})}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-x{y}^{2})}{{x}^{3}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=1-x{y}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47d515c6-7925-5320-a23c-240a300aec93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276111} {:answer/id #uuid "5d686b90-3df8-52b1-80bd-25c137cc92e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276311} {:answer/id #uuid "60c10891-1d7b-5e75-a24f-b405299f1c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276345} {:answer/id #uuid "90b73919-9597-5b2d-970e-88b31b287e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276790} {:answer/id #uuid "e26f7a61-188b-57c0-b352-c69a4a8d07d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e26f7a61-188b-57c0-b352-c69a4a8d07d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277459} :answer-field/id #uuid "b11e4e9d-db09-5e46-8d26-27def32836dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd55c472-88b5-5201-8165-4111e38766b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159752" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\sqrt{y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},4).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product, the chain, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy))+\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x))+\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1+\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1/4,4)} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4)+1+\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3c1021c-0885-5bf7-b3e5-6327c277df4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381288} {:answer/id #uuid "110927d7-7b2d-5c08-bf3d-0346fca0acbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381289} {:answer/id #uuid "60bf5676-3040-5ff2-acf5-f790c6a36101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi +1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381290} {:answer/id #uuid "92b9f3d2-b458-5615-b4f0-1138a3573da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381291} {:answer/id #uuid "d23a20cb-a2b4-58ef-a4f6-d0f3bb9feba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "110927d7-7b2d-5c08-bf3d-0346fca0acbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381289} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a23d4f6-a890-5ca9-9d9c-0ca9c46113c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39e99179-93fc-5477-adbb-fdfcbcd0de54" :question/math-academy-id "q-159753" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2}),$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) & =(2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi /4,1)} & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2})+{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "279e76f9-f9f0-5564-ae7c-148de9a68761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275840} {:answer/id #uuid "4db3fc28-5e05-5ec0-9cc6-98a0f4ab9c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276163} {:answer/id #uuid "55ba531b-b121-55b8-9aa2-75e80c2df8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276239} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad34d84-aa0e-5791-b041-c5ef3f6d8bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276864} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d1bcdc-b7ea-5e71-b840-58edcde3907f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55ba531b-b121-55b8-9aa2-75e80c2df8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276239} :answer-field/id #uuid "57d1b1f3-769f-55bc-82da-be45d6d73f43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f9cc492-b3ba-556f-a019-182cbc410bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159766" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(2,\\sqrt{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain, the sum, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & =1\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot ({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot ({x}^{2}\\cdot 0+2y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,\\sqrt{5}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\sqrt{5})} & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}+\\frac{{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5}+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{4+5}} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003296a1-5642-5e96-9e65-6300b69a9f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321150} {:answer/id #uuid "1c00999c-6574-561f-a1f1-5c0fa860bddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321358} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3bf925-d142-5810-a048-d160ed091e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321764} {:answer/id #uuid "844c30dc-5a70-5766-b0c0-94b42ca5ecd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322192} {:answer/id #uuid "f98ec2c9-ca94-5316-a3d1-a8113b2c28df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98ec2c9-ca94-5316-a3d1-a8113b2c28df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323110} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4699ed-35c4-5fe9-b9bb-a4122274633f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52ec8ecc-7425-5715-9001-87fc8bd443a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-35718" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}y,$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+{x}^{2}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (1)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =x{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faffc889-7ad7-5317-8313-d46fd721c933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{z}^{4}{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382147} {:answer/id #uuid "f262f116-4129-511f-91c6-6ef599a2213c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382148} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1efe75-1502-501f-aabc-57c0b0f621d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}y+4{z}^{3}{e}^{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382149} {:answer/id #uuid "69ad3668-cb67-53a3-8444-7e9d5683bcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382150} {:answer/id #uuid "7298654a-25b1-5bdf-8284-a89f479aed3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}y-{z}^{4}{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ad3668-cb67-53a3-8444-7e9d5683bcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382150} :answer-field/id #uuid "66ca4d90-e0e6-5e67-8e34-f7e4581124f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f2e5426-fec3-56e2-a783-8f63ba33ab35" :question/math-academy-id "q-70022" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{e}^{2y}{z}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{e}^{2y}{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{3}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-{z}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-{z}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}) & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot 2y-{z}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot 2y-{z}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}.\n$$"}]}
